{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 0.4,
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 3,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 20,
          "low": 11,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 3,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 28,
          "low": 10,
          "param": "n_iter"
        }
      ],
      "why": "Cheapest control structure (one shared micro-rotation + FSM) and the natural min-area candidate for the select rule. N does not add datapath area here, so n_iter is swept to 28 to buy angle-accuracy 'for free'; the area axis is really driven by data_width and the two guards. W starts at 11 so the search can see the round-mode borderline around LSB=2^-10 and runs to 20 to cover the high-accuracy end of the front toward the HV accuracy reference. angle_guard range lets Optuna trim angle-path/LUT width while frac_guard 0..3 covers the x/y truncation trade."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.6,
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 3,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 20,
          "low": 11,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 3,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 6,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "k"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 28,
          "low": 10,
          "param": "n_iter"
        }
      ],
      "why": "Same accuracy physics as iterative (identical bit-level path), but a different area/shift cost: k chained rotations with the FSM reuse may need fewer/cheaper shift stages than one barrel-shifted rotation, so this is where the real area trade-off may be won or lost. k is kept to 2..6 because the >=1 MSPS throughput constraint is already loose for these cycle counts; large k only burns area. Accuracy-carrying params (data_width, n_iter, guards, rounding) use the same ranges as iterative so the two families can be compared point-for-point on area at equal accuracy."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Throughput >= 1 MSPS is not binding for these families: iterative needs N+3 cycles and unrolled_k ceil(N/k)+3, and both stay under ~31 cycles for N<=28, i.e. tens of MHz of Fmax already clears 1 MSPS. The binding constraint is max_abs_err <= 2^-10 (accuracy_bits >= 10), which needs W near 12 and N near 12-14, with round-mode helping at the low-W edge. Accuracy depends only on (data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding) and is family-independent, while area is family- and k-dependent; since these families share/reuse their datapath, n_iter adds essentially no area, so I push n_iter high and let the search spend area only on data_width and the guards. Ranges straddle the feasibility boundary (W=11 borderline, up to 20 for the high-accuracy end) and cover the two HV axes (area ref 1500, accuracy ref 10). rounding is swept because round roughly halves quantization error at zero area cost. unrolled_k carries the larger share because k is an extra family-specific area axis that could hold the min-area corner; iterative is kept as the reference control structure. Suggested allocation: ~60 of the 100 evals this round at these relative shares (24 iterative / 36 unrolled_k), leaving budget for a focused refinement and the mandatory full-range front-mapping pass."
}
